<!-- PROBLEM -->
<section class="problem" id="problem">
  <div class="si">
    <div class="eyebrow">The Problem</div>
    <h2>Robots exist.<br>; Intelligence doesn't.</em></h2>
    <div class="prob-grid">
      <div class="prob-copy rev">
        <p>The world has a robot paradox. Machines capable of transforming agriculture, healthcare, and disaster response have been deployed across the globe; yet the vast majority remain locked in rigid, pre-programmed routines. <strong>They cannot adapt, learn, or respond</strong> to the unpredictable complexity of real environments.</p>
        <div class="pull-quote">"This is not a hardware problem. It is an intelligence problem."</div>
        <p>Existing AI integration for robotics remains expensive, proprietary, and out of reach for the organizations that need it most. The communities that could benefit most from intelligent automation ;; <strong>smallholder farmers, understaffed hospitals, disaster first responders</strong> ;; are the ones systematically excluded from it.</p>
        <p>ARIA is designed to close that gap. Not by building new robots, but by giving intelligence to the ones that already exist.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="prob-cards rev">
        <div class="pcard">
          <img class="pcard-img" src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1509099381441-ea3c0cf98b94?w=700&q=80&auto=format&fit=crop&crop=center" alt="African woman bent over planting crops by hand — subsistence farming without any automated help">
          <div class="pcard-body">
            <div class="pcard-tag amber">Agriculture &middot; Global</div>
            <div class="pcard-title">The precision gap</div>
            <p>Smallholder farmers have access to basic robotic equipment but not the AI tools for precision agriculture ;; crop disease detection, autonomous navigation, or adaptive harvesting. Their robots follow fixed paths and cannot respond to their environment.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="pcard">
          <img class="pcard-img" src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1648224394463-4589e018f2c3?w=700&q=80&auto=format&fit=crop&crop=center" alt="Hospital ward with bed and medical equipment — a robotic aid here cannot adapt when conditions change">
          <div class="pcard-body">
            <div class="pcard-tag blue">Healthcare &middot; Low-resource settings</div>
            <div class="pcard-title">The adaptability gap</div>
            <p>Basic robotic aids in understaffed hospitals cannot adapt to ward changes, recognize emergencies, or communicate in the local language. The potential value they could deliver goes entirely unrealized.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="pcard">
          <img class="pcard-img" src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1650638727175-347f9f3a83c8?w=700&q=80&auto=format&fit=crop&crop=center" alt="People beside a collapsed building after an earthquake — no robotic search and rescue, no specialist operator present">
          <div class="pcard-body">
            <div class="pcard-tag teal">Disaster Response &middot; Global</div>
            <div class="pcard-title">The operator gap</div>
            <p>Robotic search-and-rescue systems require trained specialist operators rarely available in the critical first hours of a disaster. A robot that only an expert can use saves far fewer lives than one that anyone can direct.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

